# Steward Binding

> **Status:** ACTIVE
> **Established:** {{ESTABLISHED}}
> **Visibility:** PRIVATE — required invariant
> **Binding kind:** `PORTFOLIO` — **1:N**, this steward binds to other stewards, not to a codebase

## Why this binding kind exists

The common steward binds **1:1 to a project**: it owns that project's vision, design and roadmap,
and implementation commits land in the paired repository. That shape assumes there *is* a paired
codebase.

A portfolio steward has none. It stewards a **program** — the arcs that run across several projects,
each of which already has its own steward. Its subject is sequencing, thrulines and cross-repo
dependencies: the questions no single steward can see from inside its own repository.

The distinction is the same one an organisation makes between the engineer who owns the mechanics of
shipping and the product manager who owns what is being shipped and in what order. Both are real
work; conflating them means the concern with tests and gates always crowds out the one without.

